findings.recommended_action:
Add baselines that matter, all scored on RPE1 with the same metric:
- a discrete G1/S/G2M class predictor;
- a readout on the S/G2M module scores alone;
- labels shuffled within phase class.
Also report a within-class or within-arc ordering metric, and base the pass/fail rule on these baselines rather than on a constant predictor. Rephrase the claims of 'phase recovery' and 'valid transfer' to say what is beaten.

review.summary:
I re-ran the analysis inline and it reproduces exactly: circular correlation +0.9832, median difference 15.1 deg, mean 16.5 deg, 43 genes. The number is stable to resampling. A cell bootstrap (60 reps) gives 95% CI 0.975-0.985 and a gene bootstrap gives 0.969-0.992. It does not change when the K562 data are renormalised (0.9816) or when only the marker genes present in both panels are used (0.9835). It also does not depend on circularity: the 12 Whitfield genes that are not phase markers give 0.992.

The problems are in what the summary concludes from these numbers:
(i) No chance anchor. The 43 genes form two separated modules (S and G2M). Shuffling genes within each module still gives 0.80 +/- 0.03. Within each module the gene order agrees only moderately (Kendall tau 0.64 for S, 0.54 for G2M), so "same gene order" and "almost perfect" are overstated.
(ii) Several of the per-line "cycle" checks are fixed by the method or sit at chance. The S peak is set to 45 deg by the code. The direction is forced by a flip rule. R near 0 is what random gene sets also give. In K562, a phase built from random genes also puts S and G2M about a quarter turn apart.
(iii) The circular correlation does not change when either line is rotated, so it cannot show that the two labels share a zero point ("same ruler"). The gene offsets are systematic: S genes sit about 20 deg later in RPE1 and G2M genes about 9 deg earlier. No acceptable mismatch for the transfer test is stated, and the Whitfield reference phases are never compared to; both lines differ from them by 33-51 deg.
(iv) The claim that the transfer will test cell biology "not batch or platform" has no support. Each line comes from its own experiment, and the two files were processed differently.
(v) The README calls a gene's peak "the phase at which the gene is most expressed", but the code computes an expression-weighted mean that is pulled toward phases where many cells sit. Correcting for cell density, or using a binned argmax, moves the headline to 0.930-0.944 and the median difference to 9-20 deg. None of this uncertainty is reported.

A supporting check I ran: projecting RPE1 cells onto K562's PCA axes gives almost the same angles as RPE1's own PCA (median 1.6 deg). So the two phase labels probably do broadly agree, but the summary does not provide this kind of evidence. Verdicts by checklist pattern:
P1 present and unaddressed;
P2 present (no simple two-module comparison);
P3 present (batch claim; HeLa reference never used);
P4 present (43 genes in two modules and one sample per line, with no CI);
P5 clean (circularity tested, not driving the result);
P6 present as a missing negative control;
P7 one pair of lines with one batch each;
P8 present (no CI, and the choice of estimator matters);
P9 present ("same ruler", "almost perfectly", "not batch");
P10 minor (no pinned versions, no data provenance).

findings.evidence:
phi is the atan2 of the first two PCs of the z-scored marker genes from the same log-normalised matrix. Scored with the analysis's own prep and circ_r2:
- the 2 marker PCs alone give circ-R2 0.984;
- the 87 marker genes reduced to 20 PCs give 0.965 (linear);
- expression with the 87 markers removed gives 0.895 linear (kNN 0.833), against 0.929 / 0.858 with them, essentially equal to scGPT's 0.892 / 0.807.
On the shared cells, Geneformer's circ-R2 error (1-R2) is 0.156 against 0.065 for expression, about 2.4 times larger.
